jira:PROJ774
    a jira:Ticket ;
    jira:id "774" ;
    jira:title "Child-Completion-As-Observation Gate" ;
    jira:section "§9 (`PRD.md:525`)" ;
    jira:status jira:Alive ;
    jira:dependsOn jira:PROJ772 ;
    jira:dependsOn jira:PROJ773 ;
    jira:evidence "**Built this session (PROJ-810 build wave) — ALIVE, adversarially re-verified.** `promote_observed_to_admitted` (`closure.rs`) takes a real `&crate::shacl::ValidationReport` (confirmed real struct, `shacl/report.rs:25`) and only promotes `Observed`→`Admitted` when it genuinely conforms — real success and failure tests, not tautological (asserts resulting state each way). **Reachability gap partially closed this session, honestly scoped.** Investigated whether `ChatmanEngine::admit_transition` has any real concept of a child-completion signal to retrofit — found it does not (grep-confirmed zero references to `WorkflowSocketId`/`ParentChildClosure` anywhere in `engine.rs` before this change). Correctly declined to force a fake hook into `admit_transition` itself. Instead built `ChatmanEngine::admit_child_completion` (`engine.rs:835`), a new, real, public S1-adjacent entry point: runs the engine's real S1 `fetch_snapshot` check first, only then calls the real `RecursiveSocketClosure::observe`/`promote_observed_to_admitted`. Personally re-verified, not just trusted: ran all 3 new tests myself (`admit_child_completion_admits_through_real_engine_s1_and_closure_gate`, `..._refuses_before_touching_closure_state_when_child_snapshot_is_unresolvable`, `..._refuses_nonconforming_evidence_through_the_real_engine_entry_point`) — 3/3 passing. `just praxis-graphlaw-test-lib 'test(chatman::)'`: 157/157, no regressions. Honest scope disclosure carried forward: `admit_child_completion` is real, non-test production code, but nothing outside this session's own new tests calls it yet — the island moved from \"test calls closure.rs directly\" to \"test calls a real engine method,\" not all the way to a production caller (e.g. an Erlang bridge). Full wiring into a real remote-result admission path remains PROJ-775's scope." .

