jira:PROJ777
    a jira:Ticket ;
    jira:id "777" ;
    jira:title "GraphLaw Authority Registry: Executable Dialect Declarations" ;
    jira:section "§11 (`PRD.md:554-588`)" ;
    jira:status jira:Alive ;
    jira:evidence "**OVERCLAIMED — corrected by adversarial review this session, this is the most severe finding of the whole review cycle.** `crates/praxis-core/src/graphlaw_authority.rs` is real, well-written source (14-dialect `REGISTRY`, name-only exact-match `authority_for`, 5 unit tests) but `crates/praxis-core/src/lib.rs` declares 13 `pub mod` statements and **`graphlaw_authority` is not among them** — no `mod graphlaw_authority;` anywhere, no `#[path]` trick, no `build.rs`. `grep -rn \"graphlaw_authority\"` repo-wide returns zero hits outside the file itself. Independently re-verified with a live test run: `just praxis-core-test graphlaw_authority` compiles and reports \"0 tests... 63 filtered out\" — none of the 5 `graphlaw_authority::tests::*` are among the crate's 63 lib tests, because the module is never compiled into any binary. Worse: `praxis-graphlaw` (the actual dialect-admission engine, `chatman/router.rs`) never references `authority_for`, and `praxis-graphlaw` does not even depend on `praxis-core` — so there is no path by which this registry could influence real dialect-authority decisions even if it were wired in. This ticket's \"5/5 tests pass\" claim was true only in isolation (a manually-scoped test target that bypasses `lib.rs`'s module tree), not as evidence the feature exists in the compiled program. Fortune-5 DoD: add `mod graphlaw_authority;` (with correct visibility) to `lib.rs`, confirm the 5 tests then appear in the crate's real test count, and wire at least one real caller (`chatman/router.rs` or an admission gate) so `authority_for()` actually gates a decision — otherwise this remains dead code regardless of test count. **Remediation this session (agent `a0d79d05654913a2e`) — PARTIAL, source-complete but compile-verification blocked.** `pub mod graphlaw_authority;` added to `crates/praxis-core/src/lib.rs:8`, matching the style of the existing 13 declarations — the dead-code gap itself is fixed in source. The 5 tests were re-confirmed correct by compiling the file standalone with bare `rustc` (the file has zero external `use` statements, so this bypasses Cargo entirely) — \"5 passed; 0 failed\" — real proof the module's logic works, but NOT yet proof it runs inside the actual `praxis-core` Cargo test binary: `wasm4pm-compat` was polled every 5s for ~3 minutes and stayed at on-disk version `26.6.5` throughout (required `^26.6.29` never appeared), so `cargo test -p praxis-core` could not run at all this session. A real caller was also found and wired: new additive wrapper `admit_manufactured_arazzo_for_dialect` in `crates/praxis-core/src/arazzo.rs` (does not modify the existing `admit_manufactured_arazzo` signature, avoiding an API-stability break for its existing callers) calls `graphlaw_authority::authority_for(declared_dialect)` and refuses via a new `CoreError::ArazzoDialectAuthorityMismatch` when the declared dialect isn't registered or isn't \"Arazzo\" — the concrete PROJ-778 escalation guard, now with a real caller instead of zero. 3 new tests written (accepts \"Arazzo\"; refuses unregistered name; refuses a real-but-wrong registered dialect) but not yet compiled/run — same blocker. `cargo fmt -p praxis-core -- --check` ran clean, confirming the new code is syntactically valid Rust (fmt doesn't type-check, so this isn't full proof). Honest caveat unchanged: this still only gates calls within `praxis-core`'s own test suite — no application-code caller exists yet, and `praxis-graphlaw`'s `chatman/router.rs` still doesn't depend on `praxis-core` (reversing that edge is a bigger architectural change, correctly not attempted). **Live-verified after the wasm4pm-compat fix (see environmental note at end of file)**: `just praxis-core-test graphlaw_authority` now shows `running 5 tests ... test result: ok. 5 passed; 0 failed ... 66 filtered out` (up from \"0 tests... 63 filtered out\" pre-fix — the module is genuinely part of the crate's real 71-test lib suite now). All 3 new `admit_manufactured_arazzo_for_dialect_*` tests also confirmed passing (`just praxis-core-test dialect` → 6 passed, 0 failed). This ticket is now fully, not just source-completely, ALIVE." .

jira:PROJ778
    a jira:Ticket ;
    jira:id "778" ;
    jira:title "Authority Registry: No-Syntax-Equivalence-Escalation Guard" ;
    jira:section "§11 (`PRD.md:588`)" ;
    jira:status jira:Alive ;
    jira:dependsOn jira:PROJ777 ;
    jira:evidence "**Was OVERCLAIMED (depended entirely on PROJ-777's dead-code gap) — resolved by the same remediation as PROJ-777.** The design itself (`authority_for()` keyed only by declared dialect name, never inspecting payload content) resists the specific near-match escalation attack, and `embedded_construct_syntax_inside_n3_does_not_escalate_authority` now genuinely passes as part of the crate's real 71-test suite (confirmed live this session, not asserted). The new `admit_manufactured_arazzo_for_dialect` gate is this ticket's first real enforcement point — confirmed via 3 passing tests, including one that specifically proves a document can't acquire Arazzo's manufacture authority by mislabeling itself under a different registered dialect name (`SPARQL CONSTRUCT`)." .

jira:PROJ779
    a jira:Ticket ;
    jira:id "779" ;
    jira:title "N3 Quarantine: Default-Off + Explicit Profile Capability Gate" ;
    jira:section "§12 (`PRD.md:592-604`)" ;
    jira:status jira:Alive ;
    jira:dependsOn jira:PROJ777 ;
    jira:evidence "**Built and independently verified this session.** New `N3Executor` in `crates/praxis-graphlaw/src/chatman/router.rs` (additive-only, ~322 lines): checks the existing `ProfileGates` capability gate first, then per-rule checks builtins against an explicit whitelist (8 pure SWAP builtins) *before* consuming that rule's declared cost against a running `N3CostBound` — proven incremental, not a single pre-declared-total check (a 2-rule prefix that succeeds alone fails once a 3rd rule pushes the cumulative total over the bound). 2 new narrow `Refusal` variants (`N3CostBoundExceeded`/`N3BuiltinRefused`), deliberately not wired into the full catalog/schemas — that's PROJ-787's scope, flagged via an explicit `NOTE(PROJ-779)` rather than a silent gap. 15 new tests, `chatman-verify` (91/91) and `chatman_static_gates` (11/11) both pass confirming no cross-lane contract broken; determinism confirmed via 5 repeated runs, byte-identical. Zero clippy findings in new code; 61 pre-existing crate-wide errors confirmed unrelated via grep. Did not touch `powl_projection.rs`/`praxis-core/` (PROJ-783's concurrent territory) — confirmed via `git status`. **Adversarially reviewed this session**: hand-traced both requested edge cases. A single rule whose own cost exceeds the bound fails closed correctly on the first `consume` call — tested (`router_test.rs:412`). A rule with `declared_cost == 0` is correct by inspection (`consume(0)` leaves `used` unchanged, still passes the builtin-whitelist check first) but genuinely untested — no `rule(..., 0)` call exists anywhere in `router_test.rs`. Low-severity coverage gap, not a defect. **Remediation this session (agent `aac0e3055ec5796b4`) — ALIVE, Cargo-verified.** Added `n3_cost_bound_consume_zero_leaves_used_unchanged` (unit-level) and `n3_executor_admits_zero_declared_cost_rule_without_consuming_budget` (end-to-end: a zero-cost rule followed by a rule needing the entire original budget, proving nothing was consumed). `just praxis-graphlaw-test-lib \"test(chatman::router)\"`: 33 passed, 0 failed." .

jira:PROJ780
    a jira:Ticket ;
    jira:id "780" ;
    jira:title "N3 Quarantine: Zero-Direct-Actuation Refusal" ;
    jira:section "§12 (`PRD.md:604-608`)" ;
    jira:status jira:Alive ;
    jira:dependsOn jira:PROJ779 ;
    jira:evidence "**Built and independently verified this session.** Found a genuine, non-artificial gap: PROJ-779's `N3Builtin` enum is a closed, pure-only 8-variant whitelist by construction, whose own doc comment says actuation-capable builtins are \"deliberately never modeled here\" — meaning the PRD's \"SHALL be refused\" requirement was vacuously true (actuation requests were simply inexpressible), not actively enforced. New closed `N3ActuationBuiltin` enum (`LogWebOperation`/`LogSemantics`/`OsProcess`, real SWAP IRIs) + `N3Rule.direct_actuation_builtins` field + a check in `N3Executor::run` that refuses unconditionally via new `Refusal::N3DirectActuationRefused` before the ordinary builtin-whitelist/cost checks — distinct from the existing `N3ActuationRefused` (query-shape level) and `N3BuiltinRefused` (mask-widenable pure builtin) variants. Deliberately not wired into the full catalog yet, matching PROJ-779/783's established precedent of deferring that to PROJ-787. 6 new negative-fixture tests (refusal fires, priority over other N3 refusals, short-circuits before later rules, all 3 actuation builtins covered, determinism). `just praxis-graphlaw-test-lib \"test(chatman::router)\"`: 39/39 passed (33 pre-existing + 6 new), run twice. `chatman_static_gates`/`chatman_refusal_governance`/`chatman_spec_theorems`: 24/24 passed, confirming the new variant doesn't disturb the cross-lane catalog contract. Clippy: 61 pre-existing errors, zero in touched files (matches PROJ-779's documented baseline exactly). No collision with the concurrent PROJ-771 agent (confirmed via `ps`/`git diff --name-only` — scoped to `abi.rs`/`router.rs`/`router_test.rs` only)." .

jira:PROJ781
    a jira:Ticket ;
